Implementations receive the absolute suite root through
`CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT`. They MUST NOT substitute repository-local golden
fixtures. Specification CI checks out pinned implementation revisions and
invokes their conformance entrypoints directly; orchestration contains no
expected protocol values.

### rc.4 manager evidence

Evidence for an rc.4 `manager` claim MUST be tied to one full immutable
implementation commit, the exact released suite manifest and `suite_sha256`,
each operating system named in the claim, and each operator-trusted Go release
family the implementation admits for `go-v1`. For each such environment, the
manager conformance entrypoint MUST:

1. verify the suite manifest and its file digests before consuming a case;
2. run every canonical and legacy schema case, including all schema 6, build
   receipt v1, marker v2, and claim v2 cases;
3. consume `fixtures/go-build-skill` from `CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT`, compare
   its context, build-source, build-input, cache-key, receipt, marker, and
   toolchain results with `expected/build-driver`, and exercise the fixed
   native `go-v1` preflight and build against the fixture on the claimed host;
4. execute every case in `vectors/build-drivers.json` and every compiled-build
   group in `vectors/manager-lifecycle.json`, including positive, rejection,
   dry-run, currentness, repair, rollback, and GC cases; and
5. report zero skipped, filtered, substituted, or implementation-golden cases.

Logs or machine-readable reports are supporting evidence; the schema-valid
claim does not replace the pinned CI run, release artifact attestation, or
proof that the exact suite executed. This requirement describes evidence for
an implementation that claims rc.4 manager conformance; it does not assert
that any named manager release already provides schema 6 support.

Specification CI pins every implementation by full Git commit ID in
`.github/workflows/implementations.yml`. A pin may advance only after that
implementation passes the same released suite in its own required CI. Branch
names and mutable tags MUST NOT be used as implementation pins.

An implementation repository may expose different entrypoint names. The
specification currently invokes pinned repositories in this form:

```text
CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT=<spec>/conformance/v1 go test -v ./internal/interop ./internal/closure ./internal/skillspec
CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT=<spec>/conformance/v1 python -m pytest -v tests/test_protocol_conformance.py  # manager
CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT=<spec>/conformance/v1 python -m pytest -v tests/test_protocol_conformance.py  # registry service
```

The release suite runs on Linux, macOS, and Windows. An individual claim lists
only operating systems for which its evidence was produced. Implementation
repositories MAY skip the external suite only when `CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT`
is absent from a developer checkout; required release CI always supplies it.

### Required failure behavior

A skipped vector is a conformance-gate failure. Accepting an expected rejection,
rejecting an expected positive case, substituting a local fixture, or omitting
the real build-fixture exercise is also a failure.

An unsupported schema, driver, receipt version, toolchain family, or policy
must be rejected without fallback. Corrupt, noncanonical, linked, incomplete,
or mismatched cache and marker inputs must not be executed or adopted. An
unprovable protected-cache boundary is a miss for a real operation,
`would-rebuild-untrusted-cache` for dry-run, and non-current for status.

Failures before build eligibility run no source-aware Go command and cause no
persistent mutation. A `go list` or `go build` failure discards private staging
and preserves existing installations, consumers, and live cache state. Dry-run
must not compile, link, repair, quarantine, publish, lock shared mutation state,
or leave operation-private state behind. Status reports non-current without
repairing it. Repair rebuilds from a revalidated snapshot; it never adopts
self-consistent candidate bytes. These effects are part of the expected result,
not optional diagnostics.

## 5. Claim format and transition

Current rc.4 evidence conforms to
[`conformance-claim-v2.schema.json`](../schemas/v1/conformance-claim-v2.schema.json).
Schema 2 fixes `protocol_version` to `1.0.0-rc.4`, permits only the four defined
classes, requires unique classes and operating systems, requires the exact
suite digest, and records only `result: "pass"`.

[`conformance-claim-v1.schema.json`](../schemas/v1/conformance-claim-v1.schema.json)
remains a supported historical contract frozen at `1.0.0-rc.3`. Readers may
retain and display a valid v1 claim as rc.3 evidence, but MUST NOT reinterpret,
upgrade, or accept it as evidence for schema 6, marker v2, build receipts, or
`go-v1`. An rc.4 implementation emits claim v2; changing only a v1 claim's
version or suite digest makes it invalid.

At minimum a claim records protocol version, implementation name and version,
classes, suite digest, operating systems, timestamp, and pass result. Claims do
not replace release CI evidence or artifact attestations.

## 6. Release gate

A protocol release candidate may be published only when:

1. all schemas compile under Draft 2020-12;
2. every example has the expected validation result;
